<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1957</h1><p class="ref-lede">1957 in world history: 26 events, 10 births, 16 deaths, 2 works, 3 states founded and 5 that came to an end. 343 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1953">1953</a><a href="/years/1954">1954</a><a href="/years/1955">1955</a><a href="/years/1956">1956</a><a href="/years/1957" aria-current="page">1957</a><a href="/years/1958">1958</a><a href="/years/1959">1959</a><a href="/years/1960">1960</a><a href="/years/1961">1961</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/harold-macmillan" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harold-macmillan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-9">9 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Anthony Eden resigns as British Prime Minister</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Citing ill health, Eden stepped down two months after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had been forced by American pressure to withdraw from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-macmillan">Harold Macmillan</a> succeeded him.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-macmillan">Harold Macmillan</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-eden">Anthony Eden</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-6">6 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Ghana becomes independent</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> became independent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a>, the first sub-Saharan colony to win independence from a European power after the war. It inspired independence movements across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-25">25 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Treaty of Rome signed</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> signed treaties creating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a> and Euratom. The EEC grew into today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-15">15 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Britain tests its first thermonuclear weapon design</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Operation Grapple saw a British bomb dropped near Malden Island in the Pacific. Its yield fell short of a true hydrogen bomb, but later tests that year succeeded and secured US nuclear cooperation.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-4">4 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Arkansas National Guard blocks Black students at Little Rock</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Governor Orval Faubus used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-panama">National Guard</a> to stop nine Black students entering Little Rock Central High School. Later in the month <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a> sent federal troops to enforce desegregation.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-1">1 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">'In God We Trust' first appears on US paper money</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The first US banknotes carrying 'In God We Trust', one-dollar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> certificates, entered circulation. Congress had made the phrase the national motto in 1956, at the height of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> anxieties about 'godless' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-4">4 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Soviet Union launches Sputnik 1</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sputnik-1">Sputnik 1</a>, an 84-kilogram metal sphere, into orbit, the first artificial satellite. Its radio beeps, picked up worldwide, shocked the West and launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/space-race">Space Race</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-3">3 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Laika becomes the first animal to orbit the Earth</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> launched the dog Laika aboard Sputnik 2. She died within hours from overheating, but the flight showed a living creature could survive launch and weightlessness.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/djuanda-declaration">Djuanda Declaration</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Djuanda Declaration was issued on 13 December 1957 by the Indonesian prime minister Djuanda Kartawidjaja.</p></div></li></ol></section>
